  "query_preview": "..At 230 AM, there are a few showers and thunderstorms\nover central Yavapai County from around Prescott to Crown King,\notherwise dry conditions are being observed. There is a distinct\narea of low pressure over northern Mexico this morning, and this\nfeature will drift northward into southern Arizona through the\nday. With well above average moisture remaining over our area, we\nexpect another round of scattered showers and thunderstorms this\nafternoon and early evening, with the highest coverage (30-50%)\nalong and south of the Mogollon Rim/White Mtns and 10-30% to the\nnorth. With the upper level feature approaching, there is a chance\nthat storm activity continues later into the night especially\nalong/east of the low. This is generally east of the I-17/US89\ncorridor. With slow storm motion and plenty of available moisture,\nthe main threat with today's activity will be locally heavy\nrainfall and flash flooding. By Friday, the upper low moves\nnortheast of our area with a drier westerly flow behind it. This\nwill bring a decrease in storm activity, with isolated to\nscattered showers/storms over the eastern half of the state.\nSaturday is looking storm free at this time as even drier air\nmoves in from the west. \n\nSunday through Monday - a trough developing along the west coast\nis forecast to move eastward across our region. Moisture\nassociated with two tropical cyclones (Hurricane Polo forecast to\nbe off the southern Baja at this time and TS Odalys much farther \nwest) will be pulled northeastward into the southwest United \nStates in advance of this trough. There is still plenty of model \nspread regarding the depth/speed of the developing trough as well \nas the location of the deepest moisture plume from the south. \nAlthough shower and thunderstorm chances will increase quickly by \nlate Sunday through Monday, it's still too early to tell how heavy\nthe rainfall might be and where the heaviest rain will be placed.\nThe timing of the trough combining with the moisture would need \nto line up just right for this to happen, but it's not out of the \nquestion. Around 10-20% of the ensembles from the latest 00Z runs \nshow a heavy rain signal (1-2\"+) for our area, though a large \nmajority show at least some rainfall. We'll need to wait a couple \nmore days to get a better handle on this so stay tuned. Rain \nchances could linger into Tuesday depending on the timing of the \ntrough, then dry weather looks to return by mid week.",
